'''''X-Men: The Animated Series''''', also known as '''''X-Men''''', is an [[Animated series|animated]] [[Superhero fiction|superhero]] television series, based on the [[Marvel Comics]] superhero team [[X-Men|of the same name]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Top 10 Comic to TV Adaptations |url=http://uk.tv.ign.com/articles/798/798709p1.html |access-date=2010-08-15 |work=IGN |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111125015738/http://uk.tv.ign.com/articles/798/798709p1.html |archive-date=November 25, 2011 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The series aired in the United States for five seasons from October 31, 1992, to September 20, 1997, on [[Fox Broadcasting Company|Fox]]'s [[Fox Kids]] [[Block programming|programming block]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Top 10 Comic to TV Adaptations|work=[[IGN]]|url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/06/22/top-10-comic-to-tv-adaptations?page=3|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308154713/https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/06/22/top-10-comic-to-tv-adaptations?page=3|date=June 21, 2007|archive-date=March 8, 2021|access-date=2010-08-15}}</ref> It was produced by [[Marvel Entertainment]] and [[Saban Entertainment]], and animated principally by [[AKOM]].

It was Marvel Comics' second attempt at an animated X-Men television series after the pilot ''[[X-Men: Pryde of the X-Men]]'' was not picked up.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Erickson |first1=Hal |title=Television Cartoon Shows: An Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1949 Through 2003 |date=2005 |edition=2nd |publisher=McFarland & Co |isbn=978-1476665993 |pages=923–926}}</ref> Set in the same [[fictional universe]] as ''[[Spider-Man: The Animated Series]]'' (1994–1998), Earth-92131,<ref>Official Handbook of the Marvel Universe A to Z #5</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://collider.com/x-men-97-mcu/ |title='X-Men '97' Doesn't Need to Be Connected to the MCU |first=Collier |last=Jennings |date=February 29, 2024 |website=Collider |access-date=October 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013164829/https://collider.com/x-men-97-mcu/ |archive-date=October 13, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> it was followed by a [[Revival (television)|revival]], ''[[X-Men '97]]'', which began airing on March 20, 2024, on [[Disney+]] to critical acclaim.

The series is currently owned and distributed by [[The Walt Disney Company]] (Marvel's parent company), which acquired all Fox Kids-related properties from [[News Corporation (1980–2013)|News Corporation]] and Saban in 2001.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Marvel Sues Disney |url=https://www.awn.com/news/marvel-sues-disney |access-date=2024-05-29 |website=Animation World Network |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013211814/https://www.awn.com/news/marvel-sues-disney |archive-date=October 13, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Synopsis==
{{Main|List of X-Men: The Animated Series and X-Men '97 adaptations}}
The show features a team similar to that of the early 1990s ''[[X-Men: Legacy|X-Men]]'' comics by [[Jim Lee]], specifically the Blue Team established early on in ''X-Men'' (vol. 2). It consists of [[Cyclops (Marvel Comics)|Cyclops]], [[Wolverine (character)|Wolverine]], [[Rogue (Marvel Comics)|Rogue]], [[Storm (X-Men: The Animated Series and X-Men '97 character)|Storm]], [[Beast (Marvel Comics)|Beast]], [[Gambit (Marvel Comics)|Gambit]], [[Jubilee (character)|Jubilee]], [[Jean Grey]], and [[Professor X]], as well as original character [[Morph (X-Men: The Animated Series and X-Men '97 character)|Morph]], who is based on [[Changeling (Marvel Comics character)|Changeling]]. All 76 episodes were directed by Larry Houston.

The series deals with social issues, including divorce ("[[Proteus (X-Men episode)|Proteus]]"), religion ("[[Nightcrawler (X-Men episode)|Nightcrawler]]" and "[[Bloodlines (X-Men episode)|Bloodlines]]"), [[the Holocaust]] ("Enter Magneto", "Deadly Reunions", "Days of Future Past" and "The Phalanx Covenant"), [[AIDS]] hysteria ("[[Time Fugitives]]"), and loneliness ("[[No Mutant Is an Island]]"). It satirizes television in the episodes "[[Mojovision]]" and "[[Longshot (X-Men episode)|Longshot]]".

It crossed over with ''[[Spider-Man: The Animated Series]]'' when Spider-Man seeks the X-Men's help to stop his progressing mutation. In the [[Secret Wars (Spider-Man)|abbreviated form]] of the [[Secret Wars]] storyline, the [[Beyonder]] and [[Madame Web]] select Spider-Man to lead a team of heroes against a group of villains. An earlier draft of "Secret Wars" involved all the X-Men, but transporting the voice cast from Canada to Los Angeles, where production for the ''Spider-Man'' animated series was based, had been too costly in previous crossovers, so the episode was rewritten to feature only Storm, whose actress, Iona Morris, lived in Los Angeles.<ref>{{cite news|last=Goldman|first=Michael|title=Stan Lee: Comic Guru|url=https://www.awn.com/animationworld/stan-lee-comic-guru|work=Animation World Magazine|publisher=[[Animation World Network]]|access-date=March 29, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220330000634/https://www.awn.com/animationworld/stan-lee-comic-guru|archive-date=March 30, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the first season, the X-Men come into conflict with human conspirators building [[Sentinel (comics)|Sentinel]] robots to exterminate mutants, Magneto's plan to instigate a human-mutant war, and the powerful mutant [[Apocalypse (character)|Apocalypse]]'s attempts to bring about the end of human civilization. Other storylines include Morph's death at the hands of the Sentinels, Beast's incarceration, and Apocalypse masterminding the assassination of Senator [[Robert Kelly (character)|Robert Kelly]] to turn humans against mutants.

In the second season, Cyclops and Jean are married and targeted by [[Mister Sinister]], who seeks to use the genetically perfect combination of their DNA to create an army of obedient mutants. Morph returns, having been rescued by Sinister and brainwashed into forcing the X-Men apart. Over time, a rift grows between humans and mutants, with the [[Friends of Humanity]], an anti-mutant group, leading their persecution. Apocalypse returns, developing a deadly plague that he plans to blame on mutants to fuel hatred against them. It features a parallel narrative of Professor X and Magneto being lost in the [[Savage Land]].

The third season involves the [[Phoenix Force]], a cosmic force that merges with Jean Grey and turns her into the Dark Phoenix. It introduces the [[Shi'ar|Shi'ar Empire]], which includes [[Lilandra Neramani]] and [[Gladiator (Kallark)|Gladiator]], and seeks to stop the Dark Phoenix. Other storylines include the introduction of Wolverine's former lover turned mercenary, [[Lady Deathstrike]], former X-Men member [[Iceman (Marvel Comics)|Iceman]], and the villainous [[Shadow King]].

Volume 5 of the ''[[Official Handbook of the Marvel Universe]]'' lists ''X-Men: The Animated Series'' as Earth-92131 in the Marvel Comics [[Multiverse (Marvel Comics)|multiverse]]. Additionally, the plague-infested future that [[Bishop (Marvel Comics)|Bishop]] tries to prevent in Season 2 is listed as Earth-13393, while [[Cable (character)|Cable]]'s release of the cure is listed as Earth-121893.

