== Production ==
In March 1990, [[Margaret Loesch]] (who had previously worked as president and chief executive officer at [[Marvel Productions]]) became head of [[Fox Children's Network]].<ref name="Quenton Hodge-Gerald">{{cite news |date=July 16, 2009 |title=Kids vet Margaret Loesch to run Hasbro-Discovery cable network |url=https://latimesblogs.latimes.com/entertainmentnewsbuzz/2009/07/kids-vet-margaret-loesch-to-run-hasbrodiscovery-cable-network.html |access-date=11 May 2011 |newspaper=Los Angeles Times |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220408064019/https://latimesblogs.latimes.com/entertainmentnewsbuzz/2009/07/kids-vet-margaret-loesch-to-run-hasbrodiscovery-cable-network.html |archive-date=April 8, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> Having championed the ''[[Pryde of the X-Men]]'' [[Television pilot|pilot]] in 1989, she ordered a five-episode miniseries of ''X-Men'' in April 1990 from Marvel Productions.<ref>{{cite news|last=de Moraes |first=Lisa|title=Marvel's X-men headed to Fox|url=https://cdnc.ucr.edu/?a=d&d=TOKS19900420.1.21&srpos=19&e=-------en--20--1--txt-txIN-%22Marvel+Productions%22-------|access-date=October 22, 2026|periodical=Thousand Oaks Star|date=April 20, 1990 |archive-url= |archive-date= |page=C-7 |format= |url-status= }}</ref><ref name="Scorch">{{cite news |last=Mangels |first=Andy |date=August 1993 |title=Scorching the Screen |work=[[Wizard (magazine)|Wizard]]: X-Men Turn Thirty |pages=70–73}}</ref> Due to Marvel Productions' work on other animated series (e. g. [[Attack of the Killer Tomatoes (TV series)|Attack of the Killer Tomatoes]], [[Little Shop]] of Fox), [[Saban Entertainment]] was contracted to produce the show. Because, at the time, they lacked sufficient staff to handle in-house production, [[Graz Entertainment]] was hired to produce episodes in 1992.

In 1991, show was given [[working title]] of ''The Uncanny X-Men''.<ref>{{cite news|last= |first=|title=Fox chases kids|url=https://www.worldradiohistory.com/hd2/IDX-Business/Magazines/Archive-BC-IDX/91-OCR/BC-1991-12-30-OCR-Page-0036.pdf#search=%22x-men%22|access-date=October 31, 2026|periodical=Broadcasting|date=December 30, 1991 |archive-url= |archive-date= |page=34 |format= |url-status= }}</ref> Mark Edward Edens and Eric Lewald were to write the show, with the two dividing the job between Edens as head writer and Lewald as story editor.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 20, 2024 |title=30 years ago, 'X-Men: The Animated Series' changed Marvel forever [Oral History] |url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/x-men-the-animated-series-pilot-oral-history |website=Inverse |access-date=November 3, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251108043348/https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/x-men-the-animated-series-pilot-oral-history |archive-date=November 8, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The voice work was done through [[Canada|Canadian]] studios, and [[South Korea]]n studio [[AKOM]] was hired to animate episodes. ''X-Men'' was initially set to premiere over [[Labor Day (United States)|Labor Day]] weekend in September; however, due to production delays, it was delayed to the end of October. When AKOM turned in the first episode, it contained several animation errors, which they refused to fix. Because of time constraints, the episode was aired unfinished;<ref name="Scorch" /> when Fox re-aired the pilot in early 1993, the errors were corrected.<ref>{{cite web |title=DRG4's Exclusive X-Men Cartoon Pilot Differences |url=http://drg4.wariocompany.com/xpilot.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080119113809/http://drg4.wariocompany.com/xpilot.html |archive-date=2008-01-19 |access-date=2008-01-17 |website=drp4.wariocompany.com}}</ref> The second episode was submitted just before the deadline, with 50 scenes missing and a single day reserved for editing.<ref name="Scorch" /> The two-part episode "Night of the Sentinels" originally aired as a "sneak preview" on October 31.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Mangels |first=Andy |date=January 1993 |title=Hollywood Heroes |journal=[[Wizard (magazine)|Wizard]] |publisher=[[Wizard Entertainment]] |issue=17 |page=32}}</ref>

Because of the production delays and animation errors, Fox threatened to sever AKOM's contracts.<ref name="Scorch" /> The series earned top ratings throughout its first season,<ref name="Scorch" /> and was renewed for a second season of 13 episodes. Throughout its run, producers had to deal with quality control issues, including attempts to cut costs and requests to change the tone of the series to more child-friendly and integrated toys.<ref name="HR25" />

The show was originally planned to run for 65 episodes, but as a result of its success, Saban funded 11 more episodes, albeit with a reduced budget due to Marvel's bankruptcy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moore |first1=Rose |date=March 23, 2016 |title=10 Things You Didn't Know About X-Men The Animated Series |url=https://screenrant.com/x-men-animated-series-best-facts-trivia/ |access-date=September 3, 2018 |work=Screen Rant |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170210192832/https://screenrant.com/x-men-animated-series-best-facts-trivia/ |archive-date=February 10, 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The series is currently owned and distributed by [[The Walt Disney Company]] (Marvel's parent company), which acquired all Fox Kids-related properties from [[News Corporation (1980–2013)|News Corporation]] and [[Saban Entertainment|Saban International]] in 2001.<ref name=":0" />

The series was added to streaming service [[Disney+]] following its launch on November 12, 2019, with a revival, ''[[X-Men '97]]'', subsequently announced to be in development.<ref>{{cite web |last1=White |first1=Brett |date=27 November 2019 |title=Every Single X-Men Animated Appearance on Disney+, in Order |url=https://decider.com/2019/11/27/x-men-cartoon-on-disney-plus-viewing-order/ |access-date=29 November 2019 |website=Decider |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201024235748/https://decider.com/2019/11/27/x-men-cartoon-on-disney-plus-viewing-order/ |archive-date=October 24, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Adams |first=Tim |date=14 October 2019 |title=X-Men, Spider-Man & More Animated Series Confirmed for Disney+ Launch Day |url=https://www.cbr.com/x-men-spider-man-animated-series-disney-plus-launch-day/ |access-date=29 November 2019 |publisher=[[Comic Book Resources]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112043900/https://www.cbr.com/x-men-spider-man-animated-series-disney-plus-launch-day/ |archive-date=November 12, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref> The series premiered on March 20, 2024.

==Other versions==
The original opening sequence, used throughout the first four seasons, features the X-Men demonstrating their mutant abilities to an instrumental theme written by [[Ron Wasserman]] and composed by [[Haim Saban]] and [[Shuki Levy]]. A modified version is introduced in the fifth season. When the series was run on weekday syndication, an alternate credits sequence was used: a high-quality Japanese-animated version of the original opening. This modified version occasionally appears in the digital streaming release of the show, which was used for re-runs on [[UPN]] on Sunday mornings, and on Toon Disney.

In Italy, where the series began airing in 1994 on [[Canale 5]], the intro and outro sequences were replaced by a new sequence and theme song: "{{lang|it|Insuperabili X-Men}}", sung by Marco Destro and Pietro Ubaldi.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.antoniogenna.net/doppiaggio/anim/xmen.htm |title=AntonioGenna.net presenta: IL MONDO DEI DOPPIATORI - ZONA ANIMAZIONE: "X-Men" |access-date=2022-08-19 |website=Il Mondo dei doppiatori |last=Genna |first=Antonio |language=it |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160619213743/http://www.antoniogenna.net/doppiaggio/anim/xmen.htm |archive-date=June 19, 2016 |url-status=live}}</ref>

''X-Men'' originally aired on [[TV Tokyo]] from 1994 to 1995. For the TV Tokyo dub of the series, the intro was replaced with a new, Japanese-animated sequence and a new theme: "Rising" by Ambience. Starting with episode 42, a second intro was used, featuring the song {{Nihongo|"Dakishimetai Dare Yori Mo"|抱きしめたい誰よりも||"I want to embrace you more than anyone else"}}. The end credits sequence was also changed: it featured shots of American ''X-Men'' comic books set to the song "Back to You", also by Ambience.

The TV Tokyo dub was directed by Yoshikazu Iwanami and featured scripts rewritten to include a more humorous, self-satirical tone with an emphasis on comical adlibbing, a hallmark of his dubbing style. Episodes were edited for time so that new segments could be added to the end to promote ''[[X-Men: Children of the Atom (video game)|X-Men: Children of the Atom]]'', which featured the dub actors pretending to play the game as their characters. A second dub was made in the early 2000s for broadcast on [[Toon Disney|Toon Disney (Japan)]] that is more faithful to the original English scripts and does not cut episodes for time. This version used the original American intro and end credits rather than the unique ones created for the TV Tokyo version.

Two versions of the episode "No Mutant is an Island" exist, each with a different animation. The first version was aired for Toon Disney reruns, can be seen on digital streaming services such as Amazon Prime Video and Disney+, aired on Fox Kids in the United States, and uses the remixed intro theme from Season 5. The second version is available on Region 1 DVD, aired on overseas Fox Kids channels, and uses the default intro theme from Seasons 1–4.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://drg4.dancemania-ex.com/xnomutant1.html |title=Animation Changes in No Mutant Is An Island |website=DRG4's Marvel Cartoon Pages |access-date=October 13, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516091903/http://drg4.dancemania-ex.com/xnomutant1.html |archive-date=May 16, 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Viewership==
In its prime, ''X-Men'' garnered very high [[Nielsen Ratings|ratings]] for a Saturday morning cartoon and received praise for adapting many different storylines from the comics. Haim Saban credits the success of the series in assisting him to sell his next project to Fox: ''[[Mighty Morphin Power Rangers]]''.<ref name="HR25" />

''X-Men'' reached a viewership of over {{nowrap|23 million}} households.<ref>{{cite magazine |title=X-Men: Children of the Atom |magazine=RePlay |date=December 1994 |volume=20 |issue=3 |page=8 |url=https://archive.org/details/re-play-volume-20-issue-no.-3-december-1994/RePlay%20-%20Volume%2020%2C%20Issue%20No.%203%20-%20December%201994/page/n7}}</ref>

==Legacy==

===''X-Men '97''===
{{Main|X-Men '97}}
By 2019, there were ongoing talks with [[Disney+]] to revive the series.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marshall |first=Andrew |date=June 10, 2019 |title=X-Men: The Animated Series Creators Want to Revive Show With Disney |url=https://screenrant.com/xmen-animated-series-disney-revival-show/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190611130310/https://screenrant.com/xmen-animated-series-disney-revival-show/ |archive-date=June 11, 2019 |access-date=November 14, 2021 |website=[[Screen Rant]]}}</ref> In November 2021, it was revealed that a revival titled ''[[X-Men '97]]'' was in development which will continue the plot of the series.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Couch |first=Aaron |date=November 12, 2021 |title=Disney+ Orders '90s 'X-Men' Revival, 'Marvel Zombies' and 'Spider-Man: Freshman Year' |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/disney-orders-90s-x-men-revival-marvel-zombies-and-spider-man-freshman-year-1235046741/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112170819/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/disney-orders-90s-x-men-revival-marvel-zombies-and-spider-man-freshman-year-1235046741/ |archive-date=November 12, 2021 |access-date=November 12, 2021 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> ''[[X-Men '97]]'' eventually premiered on March 20, 2024. Beau DeMayo served as [[head writer]] for the first two seasons, with most of the surviving cast members of the original series reprising their roles, including Dodd, Zann, Buza, Disher, Potter, Sealy-Smith, Hough, and Britton. They were joined by [[Jennifer Hale]], [[Ray Chase (voice actor)|Ray Chase]], Matthew Waterson, [[J. P. Karliak|JP Karliak]], Holly Chou, [[Jeff Bennett]], and [[A.J. LoCascio]]. Alyson Court did not reprise her role as Jubilee, requesting that Jubilee be voiced by an Asian actress. She instead returned to voice an alternate version of the character named [[Abscissa (character)|Abscissa]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Patches |first=Matt |date=November 12, 2021 |title=New X-Men cartoon set in the '90s Animated Series continuity coming to Disney Plus |url=https://www.polygon.com/22778151/x-men-97-cartoon-disney-plus-90s-animated-series-sequel |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112170146/https://www.polygon.com/22778151/x-men-97-cartoon-disney-plus-90s-animated-series-sequel |archive-date=November 12, 2021 |access-date=November 13, 2021 |website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]]}}</ref> The series is produced by [[Marvel Studios Animation]], but does not take place within the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gartenberg |first=Chaim |date=November 12, 2021 |title=Marvel embraces the Sad Wolverine meme to announce X-Men '97, a new animated Disney Plus show |url=https://www.theverge.com/2021/11/12/22778385/marvel-disney-plus-x-men-animated-series-97-sad-wolverine-meme-2023 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211112173822/https://www.theverge.com/2021/11/12/22778385/marvel-disney-plus-x-men-animated-series-97-sad-wolverine-meme-2023 |archive-date=November 12, 2021 |access-date=November 13, 2021 |website=[[The Verge]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bacon |first=Thomas |date=March 5, 2022 |title=Is X-Men 97 In The MCU? Disney+ Mutant Debut Explained |url=https://screenrant.com/xmen-97-mcu-canon-disney-plus-mutant-show/ |access-date=May 2, 2022 |website=[[Screen Rant]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220502200436/https://screenrant.com/xmen-97-mcu-canon-disney-plus-mutant-show/ |archive-date=May 2, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

