===Books===

====''Previously on X-Men''====
In 2017, series developer and showrunner Eric Lewald released the book ''Previously on X-Men: The Making of an Animated Series'', which features his interviews with 36 of the staff and voice cast behind the TV series, as well as Lewald's personal experiences on the series' development and production.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.jacobsbrownmediagroup.com/previously-on-x-men.html |title=Previously on X-Men: The Making of an Animated Series |publisher=Jacobs Brown Media Group |access-date=December 14, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191214090453/http://www.jacobsbrownmediagroup.com/previously-on-x-men.html |archive-date=December 14, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

====''X-Men: The Art and Making of The Animated Series''====
In 2020, Eric Lewald and Julia Lewald released the book ''X-Men: The Art and Making of The Animated Series'', which features previously unseen concept art, storyboards, character models, background layouts, animation cels, and other production/promotional materials, along with new interviews with the series principal artists and production staff.<ref>{{harvnb|Lewald|Lewald|2020}}</ref>

===Video games===
{{See also|Marvel vs. Capcom|l1=Marvel vs. Capcom franchise}}
* ''X-Men Cartoon Maker'': a recreational software package that allows the user to create limited animations from a library of backdrops, animations, and sound effects from the show. Wolverine and Storm appear as tutors.
* Capcom's ''VS. Series'': the characters in the series were licensed by Capcom and were the inspiration for the video game ''[[X-Men: Children of the Atom (video game)|X-Men: Children of the Atom]]'', which in turn would be the basis for the ''[[Marvel vs. Capcom (series)|Marvel vs. Capcom]]'' sub-series of video games.{{citation needed|date=February 2019}} Most of the voice actors who did the voices in the series reprised their roles for the video game.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Hot at the Arcades|magazine=[[GamePro]]|issue=67|publisher=[[International Data Group|IDG]]|date=February 1995|page=20}}</ref> Capcom would continue to use these characters long after the show was canceled, before eventually losing the rights to create Marvel-based games to [[Electronic Arts]] in 2001. Capcom, however, would reacquire the rights in 2008 and released ''[[Marvel vs. Capcom 3: Fate of Two Worlds]]'' / ''[[Ultimate Marvel vs. Capcom 3]]'' in 2011.

===In film===
The series was credited for being responsible for the beginning development of the 2000 ''[[X-Men (film)|X-Men]]'' film. Fox Kids owner [[20th Century Fox]] was impressed by the success of the TV show, and producer [[Lauren Shuler Donner]] purchased the film rights for them in 1994.<ref name="Origin">{{cite video |people=[[Stan Lee|Lee, Stan]]; [[Chris Claremont|Claremont, Chris]]; [[Bryan Singer|Singer, Bryan]]; [[Lauren Shuler Donner]]; [[Tom DeSanto]]; [[Avi Arad]] |title=The Secret Origin of The X-Men |medium=DVD |date=2000 |publisher=[[20th Century Fox]]}}</ref><ref name="gen">{{cite magazine |first=Jeff |last=Jensen |title=Generating ''X'' |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |date=July 21, 2000 |url=https://ew.com/article/2000/07/21/x-men-0/ |access-date=May 9, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150505040519/http://ew.com/article/2000/07/21/x-men-0 |archive-date=May 5, 2015 |url-status=live}}</ref> The film's success led to a [[X-Men (film series)|film franchise]], which includes a series of sequels, prequels, and spin-offs, for two decades up to 2020, when the series came to an end due to [[The Walt Disney Company|Disney]]'s [[Acquisition of 21st Century Fox by Disney|acquisition of Fox]], with the character rights reverting to Marvel Studios. Potter revealed that he once auditioned for the role of Cyclops in the 2000 film before losing it to [[James Marsden]], while Buza would appear in the film proper as a truck driver.<ref name="HR25" />

In the 2022 [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] film ''[[Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness]]'', produced by Marvel Studios, the theme song from the TV series (orchestrated by [[Danny Elfman]] and credited as ''X-Men '97 Theme'') is played when [[Charles Xavier (film character)#Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness|Charles Xavier]] (portrayed by [[Patrick Stewart]]) first appears; in the film, unlike his previous performances as the character in Fox's ''X-Men'' franchise, Stewart's Xavier is visually redesigned to match his animated counterpart, complete with his iconic green suit, blue and black tie, and yellow hoverchair.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 5, 2022 |title=The Marvel Movie Music Secretly Hidden In Doctor Strange 2 |url=https://screenrant.com/doctor-strange-2-soundtrack-marvel-movie-music-scores/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220507141029/https://screenrant.com/doctor-strange-2-soundtrack-marvel-movie-music-scores/ |archive-date=May 7, 2022 |access-date=May 7, 2022 |website=[[Screen Rant]]}}</ref>

===In television===
In the ''[[Ms. Marvel (miniseries)|Ms. Marvel]]'' episode "[[No Normal]]", set in the MCU, the theme song from the ''X-Men'' animated series is played when [[Kamala Khan (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Kamala Khan]] discovers that she is a "[[Mutant (Marvel Comics)|mutant]]".<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 13, 2022 |title=Ms. Marvel Finale MCU Easter Eggs & References |url=https://screenrant.com/ms-marvel-episode-6-mcu-easter-eggs-references/ |access-date=July 13, 2022 |website=[[Screen Rant]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220713124240/https://screenrant.com/ms-marvel-episode-6-mcu-easter-eggs-references/ |archive-date=July 13, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Lawsuit==
On October 9, 2019, Hungarian immigrant Zoltán Krisko, manager of the estate of {{ill|György Vukán|hu|Vukán György}}, filed a lawsuit against [[Marvel Entertainment]], [[Warner Chappell Music]], [[Haim Saban]], [[Shuki Levy]], [[Ron Wasserman]], [[UMG Recordings]] (the current distributor of [[Disney Music Group]]) and [[Fox Corporation]]. He claims the theme music was plagiarized from the theme song to the 1984–1991 Hungarian action-adventure television series ''[[Linda (TV series)|Linda]]'', which was composed by Vukán.<ref>{{cite web |first=Erik |last=Pedersen |url=https://deadline.com/2019/10/x-men-theme-song-lawsuit-marvel-disney-amazon-apple-linda-1202756336/ |title='X-Men' Cartoon Theme Song Sparks Lawsuit Against Marvel, Disney, Amazon, Apple |publisher=Deadline Hollywood |date=2019-10-09 |access-date=2019-10-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112021312/https://deadline.com/2019/10/x-men-theme-song-lawsuit-marvel-disney-amazon-apple-linda-1202756336/ |archive-date=November 12, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref> The case was dismissed on January 9, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Krisko v. Marvel Entertainment, LLC et al (1:19-cv-09256), New York Southern District Court |url=https://www.pacermonitor.com/public/case/30400347/Krisko_v_Marvel_Entertainment,_LLC_et_al |access-date=2026-07-21 |website=www.pacermonitor.com |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260721053819/https://www.pacermonitor.com/public/case/30400347/Krisko_v_Marvel_Entertainment,_LLC_et_al |archive-date=July 21, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

